        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objective of the Bed Rest Standard Measure (BRSM) Functional Neurological Status Exam (FNSE) was to perform functional neurological assessments that provide an objective test of neurosensory re-adaptation to the period following bedrest, as an analog to crewmember readaptation to Earth's normal gravity following prolonged weightlessness.<p>
APPROACH:<br>
</b>Three neurological assessments were performed pre-bedrest on BR-8 and post-bedrest on BR+0 and BR+4:</p>
<p>(1) Neurological Signs and Symptoms<br />
-- Headache Assessment (degree of headache the subject is experiencing: 1=none, 2=resolved, 3=intermittent, 4=persistent)</p>
<p>-- Dizziness/Faintness(degree of dizziness/faintness the subject is experiencing: 1=none, 2=mild, occasional, does not interfere with activity; 3=moderate, frequent, interferes with some activity; 4=severe, persistent,interferes with most activity)</p>
<p>-- Vertigo/Spinning (degree of vertigo/spinning the subject is experiencing: 1=none, 2=mild, occasional, does not interfere with activity; 3=moderate, frequent, interferes with some activity; 4=severe, persistent,interferes with most activity)</p>
<p>(2) Motor Performance<br />
-- Gaze/Ocular Movements (degree of gaze/ocular movements the subject is exhibiting: 1=normal, 2=mild nystagmus, quickly remits in one direction; 3=moderate nystagmus, quickly remits in more than one direction; 4=severe nystagmus, sustained in one direction)</p>
<p>-- Finger to Nose Movement (subject's performance on the finger to nose touch test: 1=normal, 2=touches nose on first attempt, misses finger first attempt; 3=misses nose, touches finger first attempt; 4=misses nose, misses finger first attempt)</p>
<p>-- Drift (subject's performance on the drift test (close eyes, extend arms, palms up): 1=normal, 2=touches nose on first attempt, misses finger first attempt; 3=misses nose, touches finger first attempt; 4=misses nose, misses finger first attempt)</p>
<p>(3) Gait and Station<br />
 -- Rising from chair (subject's performance in rising from a chair, without the use of arms: 1=normal, 2=slow, 3=tends to fall back, uses arms; 4=uses arms to stand)</p>
<p>-- Standing/Romberg (subject's performance on the standing/Romberg test [feet together, arms extended, close eyes]: 1=normal, 2=sway noted, 3=catches self with leg extension, 4=unable to perform with eyes closed)</p>
<p> -- Leg lift - Hop (subject's performance on leg lift and hop test [close eyes, lift leg, hop 3 times, alternate]: 1=normal, 2=hops with eyes open, 3=cannot stand on one leg with eyes closed, 4=cannot perform)</p>
<p> -- Tandem/Heel to toe walk (subject's performance on a tandem/heel to toe walk (5 meters): 1=normal, 2=performs with arms extended, sense of falling; 3=slips/leans to one side but recovers; 4=cannot perform)</p>
<p> -- Dynamic equilibrium (subject's performance on a dynamic equilibrium test [close eyes, walk 9 meters, turn 180 degrees and return]: 1=normal; 2=walks straight, performs turn with difficulty; 3=walks straight, cannot perform turn; 4=cannot walk straight</p>
<p>A total score is calculated by adding scores from the 3 part test: (1) Neurological Signs and Symptoms (2) Motor Performance (3) Gait and Station. A total score of 11-13 is normal, 14-15 suspect, and 15, consider referral.</p>
RESULTS:<br />
This experiment has concluded in ground-based studies sponsored by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration's Human Adaptation and Countermeasures Division. Although the raw data are archived, there are no summarized results available for this investigation. This experiment was conducted during Bed Rest Campaigns 1. LSDA does not expect to receive results from this study.<end>
